  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nFriday's feature presentation was widespread light rainfall with \nembedded heavy downpours. That changes for Saturday as ridging builds \nacross the Rocky Mountains returning hazardous heat to the region. \nSatellite imagery shows this expansive high pressure building across \nthe eastern Rockies. This high dominate the weather pattern for much \nof the coming week. \n\nEastern Kansas and western MO find itself in the eastern quadrant of \nthe high. This yield upper level northwesterly flow which one would \ntraditionally think would tamper heat expectations. Unfortunately, \nlow level flow remains southerly continuing to advect warm air and \nmoisture into the region. This advection over the previous few days \nhave established a warm and muggy environment to base Saturday's \nheat. As of midnight, the dew point at MCI sits at 73 degrees which \nyields a fairly soupy overnight. Low clouds continue to reside over \nthe region; however, upper air analysis shows ridging building \nacross the 850mb level which is expected to clear clouds out, \nespecially once solar heating begins after sunrise. Heat is boosted \nby SSW flow lifting temperatures into  mid to upper 90s. With base \ndew points averaging in the low to mid 70s, heat index values look \nto quickly increase into the triple digits. Ensemble guidance \nsuggests a 30-50 percent chance of dew points exceeding 80 degrees \nacross southern and central MO. Heat index values of 105-110 degrees \nare likely with isolated spots of 115 degrees possible in that \nregion with most other seeing values in excess of 100 degrees. \nSunday, the hazardous heat becomes more widespread as the axis of \nlow level warm air advection expands. Much of the region is expected \nto see heat index values of 105-110 degrees. Dew points once again \nlook to reach near 80 degrees across much of the region making it \nfeel oppressively muggy. Overnight lows in the mid to upper 70s \nbring little relief from the daytime heat. Hazardous heat continues \nMonday were once again heat index values of 100-110 are possible.\n\nThere are some uncertainties with heat index values Sunday through \nTuesday. Ridging across the CONUS during the summer oftentimes \nresults in thunderstorm complexes running around the periphery of the \nridge (mainly during the overnight) which can play havoc on daily \ntemperature forecasts. Some models are suggesting the potential for \na few of these thunderstorm complexes moving through the area which \ncould affect the temperature outlook. However, general consensus \nacross model members maintains the hazardous heat through the \nperiod. Higher confidence lies within the consensus as the placement \nof the high across the Rockies with generally NW flow is less \nsuitable for moisture transport. The most likely facilitator of \nmoisture in this synoptic configuration is the west coast which most \nof the water would be orographically squeezed out as it transits the \nRockies.\n\nLong range guidance suggest that some embedded upper level \nshortwaves may encourage the development of mid to low level lee \ntroughs which look to disrupt the low level flow tipping the scales \nin favor of drier air working its way into the region. Highs tamper \nslightly back to the low 90s, but dew points fall significantly to \naround 70 degrees; although, it will still feel quite unpleasant. \nThis will lower heat index expectations to around 100 which will \nfeel relatively better than the 110 from earlier in the week. \nPrecipitation chances remain present, especially late week, but \nmodel solutions remain quite divergent in timing and\nprobabilities.",
